// One extremely useful feature is the ability to run a relatively arbitrary set of code
// when getting/setting the value of a variable. Or to make the value of the variable depend
// on some pre-processing. And this applies when getting the value of it, as an rvalue, as well
// as when returning as an lvalue ref.
// This feature is present in languages like C#. For example, you can do something like:
//
//     private string name;
//     public string Name   // property
//     {
//         get { return name; }   // get method
//         set { name = value; }  // set method
//     }

// Currently, variables are defined like this:
x : int = 5;

// I dont think I want to make nontrivial variable declaration the same as C#.
// It wouldnt fit the existing syntax well. If it did use it, it would be something like:
someNontrivial : int = {
    get { return x; };
    set { return x; };
}
// But this is possibly confusing with the existing scope syntax. You can already do:
y : int = {
    result 5;
}
// So inferring that it is a nontrivial variable based on whether or not it is followed by
// a scope body doesnt work very well.

// Alternatively, I could make it be defined like modules/structs etc, with a keyword:
someNontrivial :: nontrivial{
    get { return x; };
    set { return x; };
}
// But that just makes more questions, like what are `get` and `set` in this context, 
// and where to put the actual type? If ironed out this could be good, especially using
// the compile time set :: to show that the nontrivial object itself is immutable. But
// I dont know if it even will be immutable.


// One solution that I think has the fewest problems is defining a custom type with only
// a small amount of special handling in the compiler itself, for implicitly calling get and set.
// Then the rest is just using existing features. Like:

// Definition of nontrivial type itself:
nontrivial<T : type> :: struct{
    val : T = default;

    @replaceable: @inline:
    get :: ref T(){
        return val;
    }

    @replaceable: @inline:
    set :: T(value : any){
        val = value;
        return val;
    }
}

// Usage:
x : int = 5;
foo : nontrivial<int> = {
    get = { return x + 5; },
    set = { return (x = value); }
}

foo = 2; // Implicitly calls `foo.set(2)`, changing x to 2
printl(foo); // Implicitly calls `foo.get()`, returning x+5, which is 7, and calling `printl(7)`



// This method follows the same style you would expect. It is basically just a struct
// wrapper for whatever type you want, that allows custom and implicit get and set
// It will require 3 things that must be implemented first: runtime redefinable function bodies,
// struct brace construction, and named value setting (like constructing a struct{x : int; y : int;} like {y = 5})


// I could also do something like this temporarily, but it is definitely messier:
foo : nontrivial<int> = default;
foo.get :: ref foo.T(){ return val + 5; }
foo.set :: foo.T(value : any){ return (val = value); }

foo = 10;
printl(foo); // Prints 15


// Or, I might make `get` and `set` be lambdas. But those arent implemented yet so I don't
// really know what it would look like. I just dont know if I want to allow changing
// function bodies with the regular set operator. I might prefer something like:
nontrivial<T : type> :: struct{
    val : T = default;

    get : auto = ref T(){ return val; };
    set : auto = T(value : any){ return (val = value); };
}



// Then `get` and `set` are just redefinable variables like any normal member.
// I dont know if this is how the lambda type will be written, it is just an example.
// But this would make the constructor member name initialization used above more logical.

